  "character": {
    "base": [
      "A young diocese in a state that was Catholic before it was Texan: Spanish names on the old missions, Czech and German names on the painted churches, and a city that doubled while the chancery was writing the plan for it.",
      "Austin, Waco, Temple and Killeen, Bryan and College Station, and a hundred miles of Hill Country and Blackland in between. The capitol, two universities with chaplaincies that send more men to seminary than most dioceses do, and Fort Cavazos.",
      "The presbyterate is young, half of it from somewhere else, and stretched over counties that take an afternoon to cross. The question is not what you believe but whether you can say Mass in two languages and keep a mission church open on Saturday night."
    ],
    "byNeed": {
      "critically_short": "A man is a pastor at four years ordained with two mission churches and a school, because the alternative is a deacon and a communion service, and the chancery says so without apology.",
      "stretched": "Every priest has a second job: a mission church, a hospital, a campus, the Spanish Masses at the parish down the road. The vicar for clergy keeps a map with pins.",
      "adequate": "For once the ordinations have kept pace with the funerals and the new parishes, and the chancery is talking about a vocations director who does nothing else.",
      "deep_bench": "The campus chaplaincies have done what they do, and the diocese has men to spare for the first time in its history; it is not sure what to do with them."
    },
    "byFinancial": {
      "healthy": "The money is in the suburbs, and the suburbs are new and generous, and the chancery's campaign for the schools and the retired priests came in over its goal.",
      "strained": "The growth costs more than it gives: every new parish is a mortgage and a portable building, and the old parishes in Waco and East Austin are kept open on the diocese's account.",
      "crisis": "A settlement nobody talks about, a cathedral campaign that stalled, and a chancery that has learned to say no to every pastor with a roof."
    },
    "byTension": {
      "one_voice": "Nobody here has time for a faction; the presbyterate argues about the Spanish Mass schedule and the price of diesel, and goes to the same retreat.",
      "quietly_split": "The men from the old Czech and German parishes and the men from the campus chaplaincies pray the same Office and vote differently at the presbyteral council, politely.",
      "openly_divided": "The capitol is across the street from the cathedral, and the diocese has learned to read its own clergy by which Mass they say and which legislator they lunch with."
    }
  },
  "complications": [
    "Williamson and Hays counties adding a parish's worth of Catholics every year, and a building plan that assumes a priest for each of them.",
    "Fort Cavazos: a parish of soldiers that empties and fills with every deployment, and the military chaplaincy taking two of the diocese's priests at a time.",
    "The painted churches of the Blackland, Czech and German and a century old, with three hundred families and no priest to spare, and a plan to cluster them that no one has said aloud.",
    "A capital campaign for a new cathedral that has been announced twice and begun once."
  ],
  "hiddenComplications": [
    "A settlement in a case from the 1980s that the chancery is paying in instalments the deans have not been told about.",
    "A bishop whose name is on the nuncio's list for a larger see, who is governing as if he will be here for twenty years and planning as if he will be gone in two.",
    "A campus chaplaincy that produces a third of the diocese's vocations and a chaplain the bishop cannot move without losing them."
  ],
  "seminaryName": "St. Mary's Seminary, in Houston",
